# --param msil_roundtrip=1 needs the .NET ildasm and ilasm at the version
# that msil/utils/tools/msil-tools.proj pins (MsilILAsmVersion), and without
# them lit.cfg.py stops instead of running %{msil_c} and %{msil_ll} without
# the round trip: a nested lit whose NuGet global-packages folders are all
# empty exits with lit's fatal-error status 2 and names the version and
# MsilILAsmVersion. lit.cfg.py searches $NUGET_PACKAGES, the folder that
# 'dotnet nuget locals global-packages --list' reports (dotnet itself puts
# $NUGET_PACKAGES first) and ~/.nuget/packages, so the nested lit runs with
# $NUGET_PACKAGES and the home directory ($USERPROFILE on Windows, $HOME
# elsewhere) set to an empty directory. --show-tests ends the nested lit
# after discovery even if the error were missing, so nothing in
# ../Inputs/nested-ok runs. roundtrip-composites.test runs the round trip
# where the tools are present.

# RUN: rm -rf %t.empty
# RUN: mkdir -p %t.empty
# RUN: env NUGET_PACKAGES=%t.empty USERPROFILE=%t.empty HOME=%t.empty %{harness_check} exits 2 %{harness_lit} --show-tests --param msil_roundtrip=1 %S/../Inputs/nested-ok > %t.log 2>&1
# RUN: %{harness_check} contains %t.log "fatal: msil: --param msil_roundtrip=1 needs the \.NET ildasm and ilasm %{legs_tools_version} \(MsilILAsmVersion of msil/utils/tools/msil-tools\.proj\) in the NuGet global-packages folder"
